Q4 – Choosing a Map for a Pharmacy Inventory
1. Introduction
A hospital pharmacy maintains medicine stock using medicine codes. Pharmacists frequently search for medicines, and the system generates two reports every evening:
1. A report of medicines sorted by medicine code.
2. A report of medicines in the order they were added.
Java provides three important Map implementations:
- HashMap: Stores key-value pairs and provides fast average lookup, but does not guarantee ordering.
- TreeMap: Stores entries in sorted key order.
- LinkedHashMap: Maintains insertion order while storing key-value pairs.
2. Expected Performance Before Implementation
Operation	HashMap	TreeMap	LinkedHashMap
put()	O(1) average	O(log n)	O(1) average
get()	O(1) average	O(log n)	O(1) average
Iteration	O(n)	O(n)	O(n)
Code-order report	Requires sorting	Naturally sorted	Requires sorting
Insertion-order report	No guarantee	Not preserved	Naturally ordered

Which Map Produces Each Report?
Requirement	Best choice	Reason
Medicines in code order	TreeMap	Automatically sorts keys
Medicines in insertion order	LinkedHashMap	Preserves insertion order
Frequent stock lookups	HashMap	Fast average lookup

Extra work when the wrong map is chosen:
- HashMap requires sorting the medicine codes before producing the code-order report.
- LinkedHashMap also requires sorting to generate a code-order report.
- TreeMap requires additional work to reproduce insertion order because it stores entries according to sorted keys rather than insertion order.
3. Experimental Design
I would test three different inventory sizes.
Experiment	Number of medicines	Purpose
Test 1	100	Small inventory
Test 2	10,000	Medium inventory
Test 3	100,000	Large inventory

For each inventory size, I would measure:
1. Time required for 100,000 stock lookups.
2. Time required to produce the code-order report.
3. Time required to produce the insertion-order report.
4. Whether the generated reports follow the correct order.
Prediction:
- HashMap should provide the fastest average lookups.
- TreeMap should provide sorted output without additional sorting.
- LinkedHashMap should provide insertion-order output without additional sorting.
- Sorting a HashMap or LinkedHashMap will require additional processing time.
4. Java Implementation
The following program compares HashMap, TreeMap and LinkedHashMap.

It measures lookup time and report-generation time for all three inventory sizes.

import java.util.*;

public class PharmacyInventory {

    static volatile int result;

    static void testInventory(int size) {

        Map<String, Integer> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, Integer> treeMap = new TreeMap<>();
        Map<String, Integer> linkedMap = new LinkedHashMap<>();

        List<String> insertionOrder = new ArrayList<>();

        // Insert medicines in reverse order
        for (int i = size; i >= 1; i--) {

            String code = String.format("MED%06d", i);

            hashMap.put(code, i * 10);
            treeMap.put(code, i * 10);
            linkedMap.put(code, i * 10);

            insertionOrder.add(code);
        }

        // Prepare lookup keys
        List<String> lookupKeys = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
            lookupKeys.add(
                String.format("MED%06d", (i % size) + 1)
            );
        }

        System.out.println("\nMedicines: " + size);

        // Measure 100,000 lookups
        measure("HashMap Lookup", () -> {
            int sum = 0;
            for (String key : lookupKeys) {
                sum += hashMap.get(key);
            }
            return sum;
        });

        measure("TreeMap Lookup", () -> {
            int sum = 0;
            for (String key : lookupKeys) {
                sum += treeMap.get(key);
            }
            return sum;
        });

        measure("LinkedHashMap Lookup", () -> {
            int sum = 0;
            for (String key : lookupKeys) {
                sum += linkedMap.get(key);
            }
            return sum;
        });

        // Code-order report
        measure("HashMap Code-order Report",
            () -> sortedReport(hashMap).size());

        measure("TreeMap Code-order Report",
            () -> sortedReport(treeMap).size());

        measure("LinkedHashMap Code-order Report",
            () -> sortedReport(linkedMap).size());

        // Insertion-order report
        measure("HashMap Insertion-order Report",
            () -> insertionReport(hashMap, insertionOrder).size());

        measure("TreeMap Insertion-order Report",
            () -> insertionReport(treeMap, insertionOrder).size());

        measure("LinkedHashMap Insertion-order Report",
            () -> new ArrayList<>(linkedMap.keySet()).size());

        // Verify report ordering
        System.out.println("HashMap code order correct: "
            + isSorted(sortedReport(hashMap)));

        System.out.println("TreeMap code order correct: "
            + isSorted(sortedReport(treeMap)));

        System.out.println("LinkedHashMap insertion order correct: "
            + new ArrayList<>(linkedMap.keySet()).equals(insertionOrder));
    }

    // Generate code-order report
    static List<String> sortedReport(Map<String, Integer> map) {

        List<String> codes = new ArrayList<>(map.keySet());

        Collections.sort(codes);

        return codes;
    }

    // Generate insertion-order report
    static List<String> insertionReport(
        Map<String, Integer> map,
        List<String> insertionOrder) {

        List<String> report = new ArrayList<>();

        for (String code : insertionOrder) {
            if (map.containsKey(code)) {
                report.add(code);
            }
        }

        return report;
    }

    // Verify ascending order
    static boolean isSorted(List<String> codes) {

        for (int i = 1; i < codes.size(); i++) {
            if (codes.get(i - 1).compareTo(codes.get(i)) > 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    // Measure execution time
    static void measure(String name, Runnable task) {

        // Warm-up
        task.run();

        long start = System.nanoTime();

        task.run();

        long end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println(name + ": "
            + (end - start) + " ns");
    }

    static void measure(String name, java.util.function.IntSupplier task) {

        // Warm-up
        result = task.getAsInt();

        long start = System.nanoTime();

        result = task.getAsInt();

        long end = System.nanoTime();

        System.out.println(name + ": "
            + (end - start) + " ns");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        testInventory(100);

        testInventory(10000);

        testInventory(100000);
    }
}
