6. Important Code Correction
For the code-order report, TreeMap should be allowed to use its natural sorted order rather than sorting its keys again. Use this corrected method in the program:
static List<String> sortedReport(Map<String, Integer> map) {

    List<String> codes = new ArrayList<>();

    if (map instanceof SortedMap) {
        codes.addAll(map.keySet());
    } else {
        codes.addAll(map.keySet());
        Collections.sort(codes);
    }

    return codes;
}


Also, keep only the IntSupplier version of the measure() method in the program. Remove the Runnable overload to avoid ambiguous lambda calls.

With these corrections, the program measures the actual difference between naturally ordered reports and reports that require sorting.
7. Scalability Inquiry
For a hospital pharmacy, I would define an acceptable response time for one medicine lookup as less than 1 millisecond (1 ms) under normal in-memory operating conditions.

Since pharmacists perform hundreds of lookups every day, the lookup should be fast and consistent.

My observations and decision:
- HashMap provides O(1) average lookup.
- TreeMap provides O(log n) lookup because it searches through a balanced tree.
- LinkedHashMap provides O(1) average lookup while preserving insertion order.
I would deploy HashMap if lookup speed is the main requirement and use separate sorting for reports. However, if both insertion-order and code-order reports are frequently generated, LinkedHashMap is a practical choice because it naturally supports one of the required reports.

The remaining performance cost comes from sorting medicine codes, iterating through entries and generating report output.
Why Is Hashtable Not Preferred in New Java Code?
Hashtable is an older Java collection.
- Its methods are synchronized, which introduces overhead.
- It does not preserve insertion order.
- It does not allow null keys or values.
- Modern alternatives such as HashMap and ConcurrentHashMap provide more suitable choices.
Therefore, Hashtable is generally not preferred in new Java applications.
