9. Alternative Concept – Sorted Array with Binary Search
Instead of using a Map, the pharmacy can store medicines in an array sorted by medicine code.

Java provides Arrays.binarySearch() to find a medicine quickly.

Example:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class SortedMedicineArray {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String[] medicines = {
            "MED001", "MED002", "MED003",
            "MED004", "MED005"
        };

        int index = Arrays.binarySearch(medicines, "MED003");

        if (index >= 0) {
            System.out.println("Medicine found at index: " + index);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Medicine not found");
        }
    }
}


Output:
Medicine found at index: 2


Complexity Comparison
Operation	Sorted Array	HashMap	TreeMap
Lookup	O(log n)	O(1) average	O(log n)
Insertion	O(n)	O(1) average	O(log n)
Code-order report	O(n)	O(n log n) with sorting	O(n)
Space	O(n)	O(n)	O(n)

Explanation:
- Sorted Array: Binary search provides fast lookup, but insertion requires shifting elements to maintain sorted order.
- HashMap: Provides fast average lookup but requires sorting for code-order reports.
- TreeMap: Maintains sorted keys automatically but has O(log n) lookup and insertion.
Therefore, a sorted array is suitable when the inventory changes very rarely and searches are frequent.
